That detail narrows it a lot. Generate-PDF jobs are idempotent by construction — writing the same file twice produces the same file — so a duplicate there would be invisible. MarkEmailed is not idempotent in the way that matters: it is the step that has an outward side effect, SMTP, and the guard for that side effect is a read-then-write.

Reading ```consumer.go:88-104```:

```go
func (c *Consumer) process(ctx context.Context, job Job) error {
    st, err := c.store.GetInvoiceStatus(ctx, job.InvoiceID)   // line 92
    if err != nil { return err }
    if st == store.StatusEmailed {                            // line 94  <-- check
        return nil
    }
    if err := c.mailer.Send(ctx, job); err != nil {          // line 97  <-- act
        return err
    }
    return c.store.MarkEmailed(ctx, job.InvoiceID)            // line 100 <-- write
}
```

That is a textbook time-of-check to time-of-use gap, and it only bites when the same invoice ID is in the queue twice. Which brings us to the second half: how does the same ID get enqueued twice?

```internal/queue/dispatcher.go:118``` is where I think the real bug is:

```go
func (d *Dispatcher) dispatch() {
    ids := d.repo.DueInvoiceIDs(context.Background(), 50)
    for _, id := range ids {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(id string) {
            defer wg.Done()
            d.queue.Push(context.Background(), Job{InvoiceID: id, Kind: "email"})
        }(id)
    }
}
```

Two dispatchers run — one per API replica, and we run three — and DueInvoiceIDs has no claim or lock, so every replica selects the same 50 due invoices and every replica pushes all 50. With three replicas that is a 3x multiplier before anyone gets to the consumer check. Then ```internal/queue/consumer.go``` runs 8 workers, and if the ID happens to be redelivered while a slow SMTP call is in flight at line 97, the second worker reads StatusEmailed as false at line 94 and sends anyway. That is your four-minute gap: SMTP latency, not a tight loop.

So there are two defects, and I want to be clear that fixing only the second one hides the first.
